/***
 * OrionDesk Atomic Evaluation Protocol Changes
***/

2026/11/16 17:45
Version 6.2 change summary (non-execution metadata)
================================================================
Version 6.2 replaces the ambiguity between existing common inputs and newly extracted Phase 1 deliverables.
It explicitly requires common_atomic_reqs.jsonl and common_negative_controls.jsonl as reusable candidate inputs, verifies them against the PDF, preserves valid IDs and supplied originals, and writes working/final copies to a separate evaluation output directory.
It defines PDF-grounded corrections, shared versus method-specific delta placement, cross-method propagation, missing-ledger handling, and recomputation from the final common output copies rather than historical files.

The six-turn limit, PDF-only normative authority, eight scored Markdown roles, effective four-cell universe, metric equations, duplication definition, English individual outputs, and no-archive rule remain in force.
Version 6.1's source-atomization audit, reciprocal FN/FP review, predicate-level TP proofs, and reliability disclosures are retained.
Existing ledger sizes and earlier scores are not fixed expected answers for a new run.
The preceding 6.1 document is preserved as OrionDesk_One_Pass_Atomic_Evaluation_Protocol-6.1.md; OrionDesk_One_Pass_Atomic_Evaluation_Protocol.md identifies the current 6.2 protocol.

2026/11/11 0:00
Version 6.1 change summary (non-execution metadata)
================================================================
This revision preserves the six-turn limit, the authoritative PDF-only source rule, eight scored inputs, one canonical TP/TN/FP/FN universe, metric equations, and the no-archive rule.
It adds: (1) a required independent source-atomization and acceptance-test deduplication check; (2) full-corpus reciprocal FN retrieval and table-context reconstruction; (3) corrected-false-negative versus genuinely missing versus partial/unresolved dispositions; (4) audited E2/TP predicate proofs and reciprocal FP validation; and (5) explicit reliability disclosures and machine-verifiable reconciliation across new audit ledgers.
Prior-run scores and observed false-negative examples are not accepted as current-run ground truth.

Note that, since introducing version 6.1 of the evaluation protocol, I have used the same atomic requirements ledger and negative controls ledger for all metric calculations under the statistical_analysis/ directory.
